<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hazael</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 842–796 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hazael was a king of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> who seized the throne around 842 BCE; an Assyrian text calls him the son of a nobody. He resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and then dominated the kingdoms of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, conquering lands east of the Jordan and destroying Gath. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a> is usually attributed to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hazael.jpg" alt="Hazael" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Hazael_dark.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hazael was a king of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> who is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. His place of birth is unknown: he was probably born in the greater Damascus region, and historians favour both Bashan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a>. He is first named in 1 Kings 19:15, where God tells the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> to anoint him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>In the biblical account, years later the Syrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadadezer-of-damascus">Ben-Hadad II</a>, probably the Hadadezer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a>, was ill and sent his court official Hazael with gifts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a>'s successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisha">Elisha</a>. Elisha told Hazael to tell the king that he would recover, but revealed that the king would be assassinated and that Hazael would commit atrocities against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>. Hazael denied that he was capable of such deeds. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, told the king that he would recover, suffocated him and seized power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under Hazael, Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> became an empire that ruled over large parts of contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> says that he led the Arameans against the allied forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Jehoram of Israel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaziah-of-judah">Ahaziah of Judah</a> and defeated them at Ramoth-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a>. He repelled two Assyrian attacks, seized Israelite territory east of the Jordan River and took the Philistine city of Gath. He also tried to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, without success. His death is mentioned in 2 Kings 13:24.</p><p>Excavations at Tell es-Safi, the site of Gath, have revealed dramatic evidence of its siege and conquest by Hazael. An archaeomagnetic study has suggested that Hazael's campaign also destroyed Tell Zeitah, Tel Rehov and Horvat Tevet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Most scholars see the monumental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscription found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Tel Dan</a> as erected by Hazael after he had defeated the kings of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. Decorated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronze-plaques">bronze plaques</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> horse harness, identified by their inscriptions, have been found at two Greek sites, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraion-of-samos">Heraion of Samos</a> and the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Eretria on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. The inscription reads that the object was given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> to Hazael from Umq in the year that he crossed the river, which must be the Orontes. These heirlooms were probably part of the loot taken when Tiglath-Pileser III took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 733/2 BCE, and they passed into Greek, probably Euboean, hands.</p><p>French archaeologists found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> bed decorations at Arslan Tash in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1928. One carries an Old Aramaic inscription with the name Hazael, and the bed seems to have belonged to him. Fragmentary ivories that mention Hazael were also found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hazael” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Hazael of Damascus</b> the Arameans took much of Israel’s territory in the later ninth century, but Joash and Jeroboam II won it back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The usurper <b>Hazael</b> then conquered much of Israel and destroyed Gath; the Tel Dan stele probably records his victories.</blockquote><a href="/state/aram-damascus">Aram-Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 830 BCE <b>Hazael</b> of Aram-Damascus besieged and destroyed Gath; the siege trench he dug has been excavated.</blockquote><a href="/state/gath">Gath</a></li></ul></section>
